1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:11,800
From colossal devices designed to save the world, to mind-expanding, world-shrinking

2
00:00:11,801 --> 00:00:17,320
machines, the evolution of mainframes, mice, and menus.

3
00:00:17,321 --> 00:00:19,800
Now, computers on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
It has revolutionized the way we work, play, travel, and communicate.

5
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:50,120
It touches almost every part of our lives.

6
00:00:50,121 --> 00:00:52,520
It has already helped win wars,

7
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:54,940
solved seemingly insoluble problems,

8
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:57,140
and launches into space.

9
00:00:57,141 --> 00:01:01,940
The age of computing hasn't even begun yet.

10
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:04,280
We're still in the dark ages.

11
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:10,140
There's not going to be a clear distinction between human intelligence and machine intelligence as we go through this century.

12
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:16,620
Most people today know what a personal computer is, and even something about its operation.

13
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,760
Essentially, there are input devices, such as a mouse or keyboard,

14
00:01:21,761 --> 00:01:26,100
the brains of the machine, the CPU or central processing unit,

15
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:29,720
and output devices, such as a monitor or printer.

16
00:01:31,640 --> 00:01:37,440
On the inside, the computer makes its magic by performing millions of little calculations per second.

17
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:42,180
Basically, its electronic circuits have only two options.

18
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:45,740
They can be switched on, or they can be switched off.

19
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:49,760
In binary code, a 1 represents on.

20
00:01:49,761 --> 00:01:52,760
A zero represents all.

21
00:01:52,761 --> 00:01:58,760
Therefore, a one in binary is also true, and zero is false.

22
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:06,760
Through these values we get binary logic, which allows the computer to compute.

23
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:14,760
A binary code of ones and zeros is all the computer knows and uses to make calculations

24
00:02:14,761 --> 00:02:19,840
and turn normal people into authors, animators, and web surfers.

25
00:02:21,560 --> 00:02:25,600
The computer creates its magic by the speed at which it can calculate.

26
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:30,820
Your average PC makes 100 million calculations a second,

27
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:34,580
but that's nothing compared to some of today's industrial machines.

28
00:02:36,860 --> 00:02:39,900
This is Lawrence Livermore National Laboratories,

29
00:02:39,901 --> 00:02:42,940
home of the fastest supercomputer in the world.

30
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:49,860
the Department of Energy's accelerated strategic computing initiative, ASCII-White.

31
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:53,880
We took, as our building block for these large ASCII machines,

32
00:02:54,380 --> 00:02:57,580
the typical IBM workstation that you could go down and buy from anybody.

33
00:02:58,140 --> 00:03:03,460
And we put 8,000 of these together to create a computer that was much, much faster than anything that was available.

34
00:03:04,980 --> 00:03:10,720
The ASCII-White machine has a speed of 12 trillion mathematical operations per second.

35
00:03:10,721 --> 00:03:15,360
And to break that down, the Earth's population is a little over 6 billion.

36
00:03:15,361 --> 00:03:21,440
So every man, woman and child on the face of the Earth would have to add 2,000 numbers in one second to keep up with that machine.

37
00:03:26,240 --> 00:03:31,300
Like a silicon sentinel, the ASCII White is devoted to the service of the country.

38
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:40,700
Lawrence Livermore National Laboratories is responsible for ensuring the safety and performance of the nation's nuclear weapons.

39
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:49,064
weapons. In 1992, President George Bush banned underground nuclear testing.

40
00:03:49,065 --> 00:03:51,400
That makes evaluating

41
00:03:51,401 --> 00:03:53,421
the arsenal very difficult.

42
00:03:53,422 --> 00:03:58,360
Now the laboratory has turned to supercomputers to run simulations

43
00:03:58,361 --> 00:04:02,540
to determine the integrity of the aging weapons.

44
00:04:02,541 --> 00:04:06,260
If you think about a nuclear weapon and all of its components, you begin to understand

45
00:04:06,261 --> 00:04:07,700
how difficult that process would be.

46
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:09,960
We have to be able to simulate every part of it.

47
00:04:10,740 --> 00:04:12,420
We have to be able to simulate the processes

48
00:04:12,421 --> 00:04:14,000
and physics that take place within it.

49
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:18,520
These simulations are so complicated,

50
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:21,520
even at 12 trillion calculations a second,

51
00:04:22,080 --> 00:04:24,080
it could take weeks to complete a test.

52
00:04:26,660 --> 00:04:29,920
The computer also has the power to model molecules.

53
00:04:30,540 --> 00:04:33,400
This helps scientists design experiments

54
00:04:33,401 --> 00:04:34,900
before going into the lab

55
00:04:34,901 --> 00:04:37,700
and understand what the results will likely be.

56
00:04:38,420 --> 00:04:42,440
The most effective way to get information from the computer into the human mind

57
00:04:42,441 --> 00:04:44,060
is by a visual display.

58
00:04:45,420 --> 00:04:49,100
A giant high-resolution bank of video displays

59
00:04:49,101 --> 00:04:52,420
turns the computer's endless stream of ones and zeros

60
00:04:52,421 --> 00:04:55,120
into a brilliant pixelated tapestry.

61
00:04:55,580 --> 00:04:56,820
We need to see the context.

62
00:04:57,020 --> 00:04:59,020
We need to be able to understand how it all fits together.

63
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:02,420
And then we use this simulation on a very fine scale

64
00:05:02,421 --> 00:05:06,180
as one building block, an element for our much larger simulations

65
00:05:06,181 --> 00:05:09,540
that cover a much larger volume of space or a much larger system.

66
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:13,540
Without this wall, we really wouldn't be able to understand the simulation.

67
00:05:15,920 --> 00:05:18,620
Ironically, as computer technology is getting smaller,

68
00:05:19,640 --> 00:05:23,280
supercomputers like the white machine still take up a lot of space

69
00:05:23,281 --> 00:05:25,260
and require special attention.

70
00:05:26,420 --> 00:05:28,240
We're right now in the electrical room,

71
00:05:28,360 --> 00:05:31,000
which is really on the other side of the wall from the computer room.

72
00:05:31,001 --> 00:05:35,500
A computer the size of this one needs a lot of support equipment.

73
00:05:35,501 --> 00:05:39,000
The computer right now draws about 1.3 megawatts.

74
00:05:39,001 --> 00:05:42,000
That's somewhere around 1,300 homes.

75
00:05:42,001 --> 00:05:45,000
It requires the air handlers, it requires chillers.

76
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Three 675-ton chillers keep the white from overheating

77
00:05:52,001 --> 00:05:55,000
by circulating cool water through pipes beneath the floor.

78
00:05:55,001 --> 00:06:02,920
The machine took the coordinated effort of hundreds of brilliant minds.

79
00:06:03,740 --> 00:06:07,980
But the ASCII white computer can trace its ancestral lineage back to a man

80
00:06:07,981 --> 00:06:13,040
who wasn't too concerned about national security, but had a big interest in cattle.

81
00:06:15,060 --> 00:06:18,980
Charles Babbage was an English scientist who invented the cowcatcher

82
00:06:18,981 --> 00:06:23,100
to keep steam trains from derailing upon striking bewildered bovines.

83
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,620
He's also considered by many to be the father of the computer.

84
00:06:29,280 --> 00:06:30,800
Babbage was an extraordinary scientist.

85
00:06:30,980 --> 00:06:32,160
I mean, Babbage was a great scientist.

86
00:06:32,340 --> 00:06:34,940
Babbage was 100 years ahead of his time.

87
00:06:35,320 --> 00:06:38,260
You can't say that about many people, but you could say that about Babbage.

88
00:06:38,261 --> 00:06:46,260
In the early 1800s, conceived of the idea of mechanical computers,

89
00:06:46,660 --> 00:06:50,140
that is, computers that would do more than just calculate,

90
00:06:50,740 --> 00:06:54,760
but could store information, could store numbers.

91
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,140
and draw upon them when it needed it

92
00:06:57,141 --> 00:07:00,000
and could print out its answers.

93
00:07:03,300 --> 00:07:06,560
Being a scientist, Babbage relied on the accuracy

94
00:07:06,561 --> 00:07:09,380
of mathematical, logarithm, and gravity charts.

95
00:07:11,620 --> 00:07:15,220
Computing these charts were people, called computers.

96
00:07:17,440 --> 00:07:20,180
Unfortunately, these computers often made mistakes,

97
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:21,900
enraging the inventor.

98
00:07:22,760 --> 00:07:27,520
To air is human, but for Babbage, being human wasn't good enough.

99
00:07:28,780 --> 00:07:34,280
He first envisioned a complex system of gears and shafts that worked together to make calculations.

100
00:07:35,580 --> 00:07:41,000
After 10 years of work, however, he tossed the project out, only partially completed,

101
00:07:41,460 --> 00:07:48,000
to design an even more sophisticated device, a machine that possessed the basic components of modern computers.

102
00:07:48,001 --> 00:07:58,920
punched cards provided input a mechanism called the mill made the calculations and the store held

103
00:07:58,921 --> 00:08:08,820
numbers like memory he called it the analytical engine the analytical engine performed calculations

104
00:08:08,821 --> 00:08:16,860
mechanically after inputting data the mechanism had to be cranked spinning hundreds of intricate

105
00:08:16,861 --> 00:08:20,460
gears, shafts, and number wheels to calculate the results.

106
00:08:21,800 --> 00:08:26,760
Babbage envisioned that the 15-foot-tall machine would be steam-driven because of its size.

107
00:08:27,920 --> 00:08:30,100
But Babbage never built the machine.

108
00:08:31,080 --> 00:08:34,360
His proto-computer crashed before he could complete it.

109
00:08:35,320 --> 00:08:38,240
A victim of imprecise tools and lack of funding.

110
00:08:39,760 --> 00:08:45,420
It would be nearly 100 years before a programmable computation device would again be conceived.

111
00:08:46,860 --> 00:08:53,460
Spurred by a population explosion in America, the next step in the evolution of the computer

112
00:08:53,461 --> 00:08:56,620
came at the end of the 19th century.

113
00:08:56,621 --> 00:09:02,540
An endless stream of immigrants threatened to overwhelm the U.S. Census of 1890.

114
00:09:02,541 --> 00:09:08,940
In 1887, the Census Bureau was still hand-tallying the 1880 count.

115
00:09:08,941 --> 00:09:14,540
Desperate, the Bureau begged for another way to tabulate the results.

116
00:09:14,541 --> 00:09:19,660
That's when a former MIT instructor named Herman Hollerith introduced a series of machines

117
00:09:19,661 --> 00:09:27,920
that would make the census count as easy as one, two, three.

118
00:09:27,921 --> 00:09:31,140
Each person was represented by a paper card.

119
00:09:31,141 --> 00:09:38,120
Individuals punched out boxes relating to their age, sex, race, and occupation.

120
00:09:38,121 --> 00:09:41,740
Census workers then fed the punched cards into the machines.

121
00:09:41,741 --> 00:09:45,240
The tabulating was done with electricity.

122
00:09:45,241 --> 00:09:48,920
Using electrified pins, results registered on a series

123
00:09:48,921 --> 00:09:51,340
of clock-like dials.

124
00:09:51,341 --> 00:09:54,960
Other machines sorted cards.

125
00:09:54,961 --> 00:09:57,140
Hollerith's machines dramatically sped up

126
00:09:57,141 --> 00:10:00,660
the processing of information.

127
00:10:00,661 --> 00:10:03,800
In just six weeks, the 1890 census count

128
00:10:03,801 --> 00:10:10,380
of 62,622,250 was tallied.

129
00:10:10,381 --> 00:10:14,580
Hollerith then went on to commercialize that invention.

130
00:10:14,581 --> 00:10:17,580
He realized that the census was a great customer,

131
00:10:17,581 --> 00:10:21,600
but let's face it, you only get your order once every 10 years.

132
00:10:21,601 --> 00:10:25,300
So he went out and drummed up business among railroads,

133
00:10:25,301 --> 00:10:30,960
for example, and he formed a company, which eventually

134
00:10:30,961 --> 00:10:35,740
became the basis for the IBM Corporation.

135
00:10:35,741 --> 00:10:38,040
Hollerith's invention was a milestone

136
00:10:38,041 --> 00:10:41,920
on the road from mechanical to digital computing devices.

137
00:10:41,921 --> 00:10:45,080
But it would take another 50 years and the destructive power

138
00:10:45,081 --> 00:10:48,460
of a world war to boot up the first true computer.

139
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,660
Before the 1930s, mechanical computing devices

140
00:10:57,661 --> 00:11:00,060
were used to keep track of the population,

141
00:11:00,061 --> 00:11:04,040
railroad passengers, and life insurance records.

142
00:11:04,041 --> 00:11:06,200
But the first electronic digital computers

143
00:11:06,201 --> 00:11:09,200
were built with a much more compelling aim in mind,

144
00:11:09,201 --> 00:11:12,500
to help win a world war.

145
00:11:12,501 --> 00:11:15,260
One of the earliest machines was proposed by the British

146
00:11:15,261 --> 00:11:19,880
to break the German code known as the Enigma.

147
00:11:19,881 --> 00:11:22,460
Those involved believed winning the war depended

148
00:11:22,461 --> 00:11:23,640
on building such a device.

149
00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:28,940
One of the people who worked to break the Enigma code

150
00:11:28,941 --> 00:11:30,800
was Alan Turing.

151
00:11:30,801 --> 00:11:34,220
His work before the war helped to establish a theoretical basis

152
00:11:34,221 --> 00:11:36,520
for the computers built in the 1940s.

153
00:11:39,040 --> 00:11:43,160
North of London at a secret installation in Bletchley Park,

154
00:11:43,161 --> 00:11:46,600
the British built Colossus, a computer-like machine

155
00:11:46,601 --> 00:11:49,700
designed to break the Enigma code.

156
00:11:49,701 --> 00:11:51,940
Colossus compared sequences of letters

157
00:11:51,941 --> 00:11:55,420
at a rate of 25,000 characters per second.

158
00:11:55,421 --> 00:11:59,460
The machine could only do this one job, matching letters.

159
00:11:59,461 --> 00:12:00,720
But it did it very well.

160
00:12:00,721 --> 00:12:06,720
Its deciphered German transmissions were called the Ultra Secret,

161
00:12:06,721 --> 00:12:09,720
the most closely guarded secret of the war.

162
00:12:09,721 --> 00:12:14,720
The Colossus essentially performed logical functions

163
00:12:14,721 --> 00:12:17,720
on the letters of the alphabet,

164
00:12:17,721 --> 00:12:22,720
on a stream of letters coming through an intercepted radio message.

165
00:12:22,721 --> 00:12:26,720
It would not decode it, but it would try a number of combinations,

166
00:12:26,721 --> 00:12:29,720
which in many ways is similar to what a computer does.

167
00:12:30,720 --> 00:12:33,700
While Colossus was breaking German codes,

168
00:12:34,140 --> 00:12:38,440
across the Atlantic, engineers labored to build another computational device,

169
00:12:39,060 --> 00:12:43,300
a machine which would directly influence the design of all future computers.

170
00:12:45,820 --> 00:12:49,160
America was a powerhouse producer of war implements,

171
00:12:49,161 --> 00:12:54,380
but by 1943 there was a critical shortage of a surprising component of the war machine,

172
00:12:55,540 --> 00:12:57,800
firing tables for artillery pieces.

173
00:13:00,720 --> 00:13:07,160
Firing tables allowed gunners to correctly aim their guns in different ranges, altitudes, temperatures, and wind conditions.

174
00:13:09,020 --> 00:13:13,660
Human computers frantically worked mechanical calculators to produce charts.

175
00:13:14,380 --> 00:13:16,460
But they had a hard time keeping up.

176
00:13:17,560 --> 00:13:21,380
During World War II, there were a lot of guns coming off the assembly line.

177
00:13:21,840 --> 00:13:25,720
There were a lot of firing tables to be calculated.

178
00:13:25,721 --> 00:13:31,040
It was a really big job, and it was slowing down the delivery of guns to the front.

179
00:13:32,580 --> 00:13:37,080
To solve the problem, John Mockley, a physicist from the University of Pennsylvania,

180
00:13:37,740 --> 00:13:43,240
proposed building a giant electronic computer that could figure charts in minutes, not hours.

181
00:13:46,160 --> 00:13:49,540
The Army, desperate for a device to help them win the war,

182
00:13:50,160 --> 00:13:54,120
reluctantly committed to the proposed cost of half a million dollars.

183
00:13:55,720 --> 00:14:00,280
Mockley and a brilliant graduate student in electrical engineering, Presper Eckert,

184
00:14:00,281 --> 00:14:06,040
set to work constructing ENIAC, the electronic numerical integrator and computer.

185
00:14:09,720 --> 00:14:14,360
Many times larger than Colossus, nothing close to ENIAC had ever been conceived.

186
00:14:15,880 --> 00:14:24,280
Nearly 100 feet long and weighing 30 tons, it contained almost 70,000 resistors, 10,000 capacitors,

187
00:14:24,281 --> 00:14:31,280
6,000 switches, and 18,000 delicate vacuum tubes.

188
00:14:31,281 --> 00:14:35,280
Vacuum tubes burn out just like light bulbs.

189
00:14:35,281 --> 00:14:39,280
In a machine that contained 18,000 vacuum tubes,

190
00:14:39,281 --> 00:14:42,280
at least one had to be replaced every few minutes.

191
00:14:42,281 --> 00:14:48,280
After two years of intense work, the team completed ENIAC,

192
00:14:48,281 --> 00:14:51,280
three months after the Japanese surrender.

193
00:14:51,281 --> 00:14:57,280
Although it wasn't finished in time to help win the war, ENIAC was a marvelous machine.

194
00:14:57,281 --> 00:15:05,280
It could perform up to 5,000 additions, 357 multiplications, and 38 divisions every second.

195
00:15:05,281 --> 00:15:11,280
The interesting thing about the ENIAC was that it had a general purpose capability.

196
00:15:11,281 --> 00:15:19,280
It could be reconfigured to solve other problems, and there was never any problem finding work for the ENIAC to do.

197
00:15:19,281 --> 00:15:23,280
All the armed services clamored for time on the machine.

198
00:15:23,281 --> 00:15:27,280
Industrial customers, university researchers wanted it.

199
00:15:27,281 --> 00:15:31,280
So it was kept busy right into the 1950s.

200
00:15:31,281 --> 00:15:35,280
By far the most complex machine of its time,

201
00:15:35,281 --> 00:15:39,280
ENIAC still lacked many of the qualities of a modern computer.

202
00:15:39,281 --> 00:15:43,280
Its memory was very primitive and could only store a maximum

203
00:15:43,281 --> 00:15:47,280
of 20 10-digit decimal numbers.

204
00:15:47,281 --> 00:15:55,200
It had to be laboriously rewired each time it was programmed, and couldn't make logical decisions based on its calculations.

205
00:15:57,460 --> 00:16:00,840
But ENIAC had proven that computers could be constructed.

206
00:16:00,841 --> 00:16:12,900
Just before the end of the Second World War, John von Neumann, an advisor to Eckert and

207
00:16:12,901 --> 00:16:17,760
Mockley's ENIAC project, wrote a paper that was to greatly affect the next stage of computer

208
00:16:17,761 --> 00:16:18,461
design.

209
00:16:20,900 --> 00:16:25,480
Von Neumann possessed a photographic memory and was one of the principal scientists involved

210
00:16:25,481 --> 00:16:26,620
in the Manhattan Project.

211
00:16:26,621 --> 00:16:32,887
project. Von Neumann's paper delineated the structure of the modern computer.

212
00:16:32,888 --> 00:16:33,620
It was to

213
00:16:34,460 --> 00:16:39,459
have a processing unit, a controlling unit, memory, input and output.

214
00:16:39,460 --> 00:16:40,980
But most importantly,

215
00:16:40,981 --> 00:16:47,980
in the evolution of computers, it would hold its programming internally in its memory.

216
00:16:47,981 --> 00:16:53,020
Internally held programs give computers their power and versatility, because an internal

217
00:16:53,021 --> 00:16:57,940
program can modify what it does based on data or the results of computations.

218
00:16:57,941 --> 00:17:07,540
Von Neumann's paper proposed a machine that truly had a general purpose capability.

219
00:17:07,541 --> 00:17:15,080
In this paper, he described the attributes of the computer, how it ought to be organized

220
00:17:15,081 --> 00:17:15,781
logically.

221
00:17:15,782 --> 00:17:21,220
That is, it needed to have a central processor where the calculations would be performed.

222
00:17:21,221 --> 00:17:25,360
it needed to have a memory where the program and data would be stored,

223
00:17:25,960 --> 00:17:29,800
it needed to have an output unit and an input unit of some sort.

224
00:17:30,200 --> 00:17:35,280
He laid out the logical plan, the map, for a computer

225
00:17:35,281 --> 00:17:37,560
and pointed the direction for the future.

226
00:17:39,120 --> 00:17:41,980
The idea for storing programs internally

227
00:17:41,981 --> 00:17:45,500
was the last key to developing the true general-purpose computer.

228
00:17:45,980 --> 00:17:49,600
But whether it was von Neumann's idea has long been hotly debated.

229
00:17:51,220 --> 00:17:58,240
Eckerd and Mockley, who had worked with von Neumann, claimed they formulated internal programs as a natural part of their work building ENIAC,

230
00:17:58,820 --> 00:18:02,400
although they couldn't stop and incorporate the idea into the machine.

231
00:18:04,560 --> 00:18:08,240
However, Eckerd and Mockley weren't included as authors in the paper.

232
00:18:09,220 --> 00:18:10,760
They felt they had been betrayed.

233
00:18:12,140 --> 00:18:19,860
But realizing the computer might find itself useful in the business world, Eckerd and Mockley formed their own computer company.

234
00:18:20,560 --> 00:18:26,520
They set to work on a machine they called the Universal Automatic Computer, UNIVAC.

235
00:18:27,360 --> 00:18:33,120
The UNIVAC, unlike previous one-of-a-kind machines built to solve scientific or military problems,

236
00:18:33,580 --> 00:18:35,560
could be programmed to serve many purposes.

237
00:18:37,460 --> 00:18:41,940
The UNIVAC was also the first computer to be mass-produced and sold commercially.

238
00:18:44,960 --> 00:18:49,360
The machine could be programmed for a variety of data processing tasks,

239
00:18:49,361 --> 00:18:51,840
such as payroll, inventory, and billing.

240
00:18:53,760 --> 00:18:56,960
Compact magnetic tape drives stored data,

241
00:18:57,380 --> 00:18:58,960
and results were automatically printed.

242
00:19:00,180 --> 00:19:04,120
Still, very few people understood how useful a computer could be.

243
00:19:04,700 --> 00:19:08,520
That perception changed dramatically on election night 1952,

244
00:19:08,980 --> 00:19:13,000
when CBS used a UNIVAC to predict the outcome of the presidential race

245
00:19:13,001 --> 00:19:15,800
between Dwight Eisenhower and Adlai Stevenson.

246
00:19:15,801 --> 00:19:20,040
Opinion polls said the race was too close to call.

247
00:19:20,660 --> 00:19:22,180
The UNIVAC figured otherwise.

248
00:19:24,400 --> 00:19:28,860
Engineers fed the computer early results of selected eastern precincts

249
00:19:28,861 --> 00:19:31,340
well before polls closed in the western states.

250
00:19:32,800 --> 00:19:34,560
Experts thought it was a tight race,

251
00:19:34,940 --> 00:19:38,820
but the UNIVAC predicted a landslide victory for Eisenhower.

252
00:19:40,300 --> 00:19:43,340
Even CBS didn't believe what the computer was saying

253
00:19:43,341 --> 00:19:46,120
and hesitated reporting the computer's results.

254
00:19:47,540 --> 00:19:50,500
But in the end, the UNIVAC was correct.

255
00:19:51,220 --> 00:19:54,000
The power and utility of the computer had been proven.

256
00:19:56,360 --> 00:19:58,420
After the success of UNIVAC,

257
00:19:58,780 --> 00:20:01,980
various companies began to see a future in computer development.

258
00:20:03,320 --> 00:20:06,800
IBM began to realize that computers would become smaller

259
00:20:06,801 --> 00:20:10,040
and less expensive and easier to use,

260
00:20:10,041 --> 00:20:14,060
and that there would be a place in business for these machines,

261
00:20:14,260 --> 00:20:19,000
and that indeed these machines might eventually replace punch card technology.

262
00:20:19,240 --> 00:20:20,180
They're bread and butter.

263
00:20:23,220 --> 00:20:29,120
By the early 1960s, IBM dominated the large mainframe computer market.

264
00:20:30,520 --> 00:20:32,860
Business was becoming dependent on computers.

265
00:20:33,980 --> 00:20:35,560
The machines were getting smaller,

266
00:20:36,120 --> 00:20:38,900
thanks to the switch from tube technology to transistors.

267
00:20:40,040 --> 00:20:45,000
And a race to the moon was about to make the computer smaller still.

268
00:20:48,300 --> 00:20:52,980
In 1961, America trailed Russia in the race for space.

269
00:20:54,020 --> 00:20:56,500
In the ever-increasing attempt to beat the Russians,

270
00:20:57,240 --> 00:20:59,600
NASA's goal was to land a man on the moon.

271
00:21:00,160 --> 00:21:01,100
It wouldn't be easy.

272
00:21:01,600 --> 00:21:03,960
When I first went to work for NASA in 1962,

273
00:21:04,540 --> 00:21:06,800
on the first day they gave me a 20-inch slide rule.

274
00:21:06,801 --> 00:21:10,680
And our calculations were primarily done on handheld calculators.

275
00:21:10,820 --> 00:21:13,880
We had a number of people that we would take our data sheets down to,

276
00:21:14,020 --> 00:21:17,160
and it was their job to sit there on these little calculators.

277
00:21:19,380 --> 00:21:24,180
But astronauts couldn't rely on slide rules or handheld calculators in space.

278
00:21:25,500 --> 00:21:28,600
The mission required a sophisticated guidance computer.

279
00:21:29,600 --> 00:21:34,020
In the early 1960s, however, a machine as powerful as the one they needed

280
00:21:34,021 --> 00:21:36,080
took up an entire room.

281
00:21:36,800 --> 00:21:40,800
NASA looked to shrink the problem.

282
00:21:40,801 --> 00:21:46,800
Fortunately, the first great breakthrough on the road to miniaturization had already occurred.

283
00:21:46,801 --> 00:21:53,800
In 1947, three scientists at Bell Labs invented the transistor.

284
00:21:53,801 --> 00:21:57,800
The transistor performed the same on- or off function as vacuum tubes,

285
00:21:57,801 --> 00:22:02,800
but was much smaller and more reliable.

286
00:22:02,801 --> 00:22:07,800
Transistors were made with silicon and tiny wires.

287
00:22:07,801 --> 00:22:09,800
Silicon is a semiconductor.

288
00:22:09,801 --> 00:22:16,800
If a small voltage is supplied, it will act like a conductor, allowing a current to pass between wires.

289
00:22:16,801 --> 00:22:22,800
If there is no voltage, it will act more like an insulator, blocking the current.

290
00:22:22,801 --> 00:22:28,800
This characteristic of silicon is what enabled transistors to speak the binary language of computers,

291
00:22:28,801 --> 00:22:36,480
and would make silicon the foundation of the computer industry.

292
00:22:36,481 --> 00:22:43,120
The next step in miniaturization occurred in 1959, when Robert Noyce and Jack Kilby, engineers

293
00:22:43,121 --> 00:22:48,600
for rival transistor manufacturers, independently came up with breakthroughs that led to the

294
00:22:48,601 --> 00:22:52,120
same revolutionary idea.

295
00:22:52,121 --> 00:22:58,720
An entire network of electronic components, transistors and all, incorporated onto a single

296
00:22:58,721 --> 00:23:02,620
chip of silicon.

297
00:23:02,621 --> 00:23:05,760
This great innovation in electronics was called the

298
00:23:05,761 --> 00:23:10,260
integrated circuit.

299
00:23:10,261 --> 00:23:14,400
In 1969, 5,000 integrated circuits made up the heart of

300
00:23:14,401 --> 00:23:19,640
each of two identical computers, one on the lunar orbiter and

301
00:23:19,641 --> 00:23:21,640
one on the lander.

302
00:23:21,641 --> 00:23:24,580
For their size, these were the most powerful computers on

303
00:23:24,581 --> 00:23:25,980
Earth, or off.

304
00:23:28,700 --> 00:23:34,060
As Neil Armstrong took one small step in the lunar dust, Ted Hoff, an engineer with the

305
00:23:34,061 --> 00:23:40,640
Intel Computer Chip Company, was making the last great leap in miniaturization, developing

306
00:23:40,641 --> 00:23:46,060
an idea that would put an entire computer on a chip of silicon, the microprocessor.

307
00:23:47,900 --> 00:23:54,460
It was the invention, not just of integrated circuits, but of a particular kind of

308
00:23:54,461 --> 00:24:00,420
integrated circuit, the microprocessor that makes today's personal computers possible.

309
00:24:02,780 --> 00:24:08,220
Hoff had been told to design 12 separate integrated circuits to make a Japanese pocket calculator.

310
00:24:09,380 --> 00:24:15,380
He suggested placing the entire processing unit on a chip and programming it just like a computer.

311
00:24:17,460 --> 00:24:23,080
Intel developed the idea, and by 1970, they had a working model of a microprocessor.

312
00:24:24,460 --> 00:24:27,520
Computers were on their way to becoming smaller.

313
00:24:28,400 --> 00:24:32,060
However, they were still a long way from being easy to operate.

314
00:24:32,660 --> 00:24:33,900
Look what else we can do in here.

315
00:24:34,100 --> 00:24:37,140
One of the earliest attempts to simplify the computer's operation

316
00:24:37,141 --> 00:24:39,360
was spearheaded by Doug Engelbart.

317
00:24:40,300 --> 00:24:44,180
The computer scientist conceptualized a simple-to-use computer

318
00:24:44,181 --> 00:24:48,060
and even demonstrated a prototype in 1968

319
00:24:48,061 --> 00:24:51,460
at the Fall Joint Computer Conference in San Francisco.

320
00:24:52,260 --> 00:24:53,360
It'll offer you a character.

321
00:24:53,361 --> 00:24:57,120
If I hit W, it'll say delete word.

322
00:24:57,720 --> 00:24:59,920
The arrow moves back and forth to give me feedback.

323
00:25:01,060 --> 00:25:02,540
My tracking spot changes.

324
00:25:02,760 --> 00:25:05,820
Wielding a keyboard and a pointing device he called a mouse,

325
00:25:07,040 --> 00:25:09,920
Engelbart worked with a computer 30 miles away,

326
00:25:10,500 --> 00:25:11,800
linked by microwaves,

327
00:25:12,220 --> 00:25:14,880
and demonstrated word processing and hypertext.

328
00:25:16,280 --> 00:25:18,580
Many in the audience went home inspired,

329
00:25:19,120 --> 00:25:22,600
but one group alone was to fulfill Engelbart's vision.

330
00:25:23,360 --> 00:25:28,360
That group was just down the road from San Francisco at Xerox PARC.

331
00:25:30,360 --> 00:25:36,360
Xerox believed the future might be in computers and set out to build an easy-to-use machine.

332
00:25:37,360 --> 00:25:41,360
In 1973, Xerox introduced the Alto,

333
00:25:41,361 --> 00:25:47,360
which incorporated many of the innovations in personal computers we take for granted today.

334
00:25:48,360 --> 00:25:50,360
The Alto used a mouse,

335
00:25:50,361 --> 00:25:52,360
a graphical interface,

336
00:25:52,361 --> 00:25:57,360
built-in networking, and it printed on a laser printer.

337
00:25:57,361 --> 00:26:00,360
It should have revolutionized the industry,

338
00:26:00,361 --> 00:26:03,360
but at $18,000 per machine,

339
00:26:03,361 --> 00:26:07,360
the Alto wasn't marketed to individuals.

340
00:26:09,360 --> 00:26:12,360
Computers were still elite machines,

341
00:26:12,361 --> 00:26:17,360
owned by the government, large corporations, and universities.

342
00:26:17,361 --> 00:26:20,360
In the past, giant mainframes processed payrolls,

343
00:26:20,361 --> 00:26:24,260
payrolls, kept track of records, and made scientific calculations.

344
00:26:24,261 --> 00:26:29,680
If a college was lucky enough to have access to a computer, students and professors shared

345
00:26:29,681 --> 00:26:31,180
time on it.

346
00:26:31,181 --> 00:26:34,920
But the chip market was growing.

347
00:26:34,921 --> 00:26:39,880
Processing speeds were going up, and prices were coming down.

348
00:26:39,881 --> 00:26:45,680
A revolution was at hand, and the charge wouldn't be led by a large corporation, but by a group

349
00:26:45,681 --> 00:26:48,480
of hobbyists and free-thinking engineers.

350
00:26:48,481 --> 00:26:54,860
Two of those revolutionaries were Steve Jobs and Steven Wozniak.

351
00:26:55,860 --> 00:27:02,300
In the mid-1970s, they started manufacturing a small computer in a Palo Alto garage.

352
00:27:03,040 --> 00:27:05,580
They called it the Apple I.

353
00:27:07,120 --> 00:27:12,500
Steve Jobs was a college dropout, but he was a college dropout with a difference.

354
00:27:12,501 --> 00:27:15,939
He was very intelligent. He had a lot of street smarts.

355
00:27:15,940 --> 00:27:17,940
He was also extremely ambitious.

356
00:27:18,480 --> 00:27:26,400
I think perhaps more important than that, he knew the other Steve, Steve Wozniak, who was a brilliant hardware engineer.

357
00:27:28,400 --> 00:27:34,480
As chip prices came down, a few inexpensive build-it-yourself computer kits started appearing on the market.

358
00:27:36,280 --> 00:27:40,640
One of the first kits appeared on the cover of Popular Electronics in 1975.

359
00:27:41,360 --> 00:27:43,160
It was called the Altair.

360
00:27:43,780 --> 00:27:49,120
The Altair was touted as the world's first mini-computer kit to rival commercial models.

361
00:27:50,520 --> 00:27:54,020
At $400 per kit, a lot of people were interested.

362
00:27:55,080 --> 00:27:58,420
These early computers, they were the first ones before the Apples,

363
00:27:58,560 --> 00:28:01,600
were really for technicians and computer engineers

364
00:28:01,601 --> 00:28:04,900
that kind of knew how to hook up wires and get other devices hooked up

365
00:28:04,901 --> 00:28:06,400
and make it work for them a little bit.

366
00:28:06,700 --> 00:28:11,180
So I thought, well, I'll just buy these chips, buy a few chips,

367
00:28:11,181 --> 00:28:14,240
get a microprocessor, and I'll design my own computer.

368
00:28:14,700 --> 00:28:16,480
It wasn't, you know, how will I outsmart the world?

369
00:28:16,600 --> 00:28:19,560
It was just, I want to make this computer more personal

370
00:28:19,561 --> 00:28:22,140
than switches that have up or down positions

371
00:28:22,141 --> 00:28:24,520
for toggling weird binary numbers in.

372
00:28:26,420 --> 00:28:28,340
Steve Jobs sold his Volkswagen,

373
00:28:28,780 --> 00:28:31,160
and Stephen Wozniak sold his HP calculator

374
00:28:31,161 --> 00:28:32,380
to finance their company.

375
00:28:34,280 --> 00:28:37,460
In 1977, they introduced the Apple II,

376
00:28:38,260 --> 00:28:40,760
an attractive self-contained computer and keyboard.

377
00:28:41,180 --> 00:28:46,240
With the addition of a monitor and optional floppy disk drive, the look of the personal

378
00:28:46,241 --> 00:28:50,260
computer was beginning to take shape.

379
00:28:50,261 --> 00:28:54,700
The Apple II came standard with 4 kilobytes of memory.

380
00:28:54,701 --> 00:29:00,480
And at a base price of $1,200, it was also much less expensive than previous industrial

381
00:29:00,481 --> 00:29:05,280
machines.

382
00:29:05,281 --> 00:29:12,420
The Apple II helped put the personal in PC by making it a plug-and-play device.

383
00:29:12,421 --> 00:29:17,780
A buyer didn't have to spend time meticulously soldering parts together.

384
00:29:17,781 --> 00:29:22,360
The person could immediately start programming the computer or buy software developed by

385
00:29:22,361 --> 00:29:26,040
burgeoning software companies.

386
00:29:26,041 --> 00:29:31,480
Hoping to take a bite out of Apple's market share, in 1981 IBM introduced their version

387
00:29:31,481 --> 00:29:38,880
of a personal computing device simply known as the PC.

388
00:29:38,881 --> 00:29:43,720
Computer sales continued to rise in the early 1980s, but before the personal computer could

389
00:29:43,721 --> 00:29:49,180
take over the planet, the machine still had to become a little more user-friendly, something

390
00:29:49,181 --> 00:29:51,900
anyone could operate.

391
00:29:51,901 --> 00:29:59,860
The early Apple computers, as well as all early personal computers, did not have graphical

392
00:29:59,861 --> 00:30:07,620
interfaces. They didn't have mice. They had what's known as a command line

393
00:30:07,621 --> 00:30:12,280
interface. That is, you typed instructions into the computer and your

394
00:30:12,281 --> 00:30:18,540
instructions appeared as text on the screen. Inspired by the work done at

395
00:30:18,541 --> 00:30:24,300
Xerox PARC, Apple took many of the Alto computers best features, such as the

396
00:30:24,301 --> 00:30:32,200
graphic interface and mouse, improved upon them, and made an affordable machine.

397
00:30:32,201 --> 00:30:38,840
Apple introduced the Macintosh computer in 1984.

398
00:30:38,841 --> 00:30:43,620
What made the Macintosh easy to use was its operating system and applications, otherwise

399
00:30:43,621 --> 00:30:47,420
known as software.

400
00:30:47,421 --> 00:30:51,680
Software is the list of instructions that tell the computer what to do, whether it should

401
00:30:51,681 --> 00:30:55,600
like a word processor or a spreadsheet or any other application.

402
00:30:58,800 --> 00:31:04,400
Increasingly, software was dominating the advances made in computers, and Bill Gates,

403
00:31:04,401 --> 00:31:09,440
the young president of a computing software company called Microsoft, was there to capitalize

404
00:31:09,441 --> 00:31:11,040
on the growing market.

405
00:31:11,041 --> 00:31:16,640
He would eventually become a household name, parlaying his vision into a vast

406
00:31:16,641 --> 00:31:23,100
software empire and becoming the richest man in the world.

407
00:31:23,101 --> 00:31:29,820
Back in 1981, IBM decided to use Microsoft's operating system known as PC-DOS.

408
00:31:29,821 --> 00:31:35,620
As IBM clones appeared on the market, Microsoft was free to sell their MS-DOS system to rival

409
00:31:35,621 --> 00:31:39,520
computer companies as well.

410
00:31:39,521 --> 00:31:43,740
IBM didn't try to hold on to the software and the hardware, so having them in two separate

411
00:31:43,741 --> 00:31:46,180
companies was great for Microsoft.

412
00:31:46,181 --> 00:31:50,260
Everybody in the world could come and build an Intel computer, and Microsoft had the operating system.

413
00:31:50,920 --> 00:31:53,840
So what happened was you walk into a computer store back in those days,

414
00:31:53,900 --> 00:31:58,280
and you would see brand after brand after brand after brand of all these compatible computers,

415
00:31:58,760 --> 00:32:00,220
and then you'd see a Macintosh.

416
00:32:02,720 --> 00:32:05,140
Imitation is the sincerest form of flattery,

417
00:32:05,141 --> 00:32:10,020
but Apple didn't feel flattered when Microsoft introduced their Windows platform for the PC.

418
00:32:11,840 --> 00:32:12,960
Windows imitated.

419
00:32:12,961 --> 00:32:17,960
Some say stole the graphic interface, pulled down menus and all that Apple had popularized.

420
00:32:18,960 --> 00:32:28,960
Well, there's this idea that Bill Gates came into Apple and copied the graphical look and the users to actually lead up into Windows.

421
00:32:28,961 --> 00:32:31,960
Because anybody, once they see it, you don't have to see it for very long.

422
00:32:31,961 --> 00:32:35,960
You kind of know this is the future and once you go through that door, you're not coming back.

423
00:32:35,961 --> 00:32:38,960
The true story is more like we needed some software written.

424
00:32:38,961 --> 00:32:41,960
And Gates had a company, Microsoft, that wrote software.

425
00:32:41,961 --> 00:32:46,960
So we had to show him a computer and give him copies of it to write the software.

426
00:32:46,961 --> 00:32:48,960
There wasn't any other way.

427
00:32:48,961 --> 00:32:50,960
And I don't know if I'd call that exactly stealing.

428
00:32:50,961 --> 00:32:55,960
It's like being an opportunist and looking in the right places and negotiating the way you want to

429
00:32:55,961 --> 00:32:59,960
and getting what you want and making something off of it even though the other guy invented it first.

430
00:32:59,961 --> 00:33:02,960
It's hard to call that stealing.

431
00:33:04,960 --> 00:33:07,960
Computer sales skyrocketed in the 1980s.

432
00:33:07,961 --> 00:33:13,760
Going from 500,000 machines a year in 1981 to 7 million by the end of the decade.

433
00:33:15,300 --> 00:33:19,840
Friendly machines and ever more sophisticated software excited imaginations.

434
00:33:21,880 --> 00:33:24,480
But the revolution was far from over.

435
00:33:25,280 --> 00:33:30,440
A worldwide communications network was destined to dramatically alter people's lives.

436
00:33:31,620 --> 00:33:35,140
Soon the entire planet would be caught in an inescapable web.

437
00:33:35,141 --> 00:33:40,140
Next, see how your home's getting connected.

438
00:33:40,141 --> 00:33:47,140
Hooking up your television, toaster, coffee maker, and microwave.

439
00:33:47,141 --> 00:33:53,140
In the 1990s, computer sales exploded, driven by an expanding world wide web.

440
00:33:53,141 --> 00:33:58,140
I would say the internet is more important than personal computers,

441
00:33:58,141 --> 00:34:02,140
although you need a personal computer to use the internet.

442
00:34:02,141 --> 00:34:04,960
It's like, what's more important, the streets or the cars?

443
00:34:08,280 --> 00:34:12,720
The web is connecting us, but it's not as constrictive as it used to be.

444
00:34:15,980 --> 00:34:18,380
Wireless technology is making us mobile.

445
00:34:21,180 --> 00:34:27,320
Already, personal digital assistants and web appliances are further changing the look and role of computers.

446
00:34:27,321 --> 00:34:36,140
microprocessors are everywhere the line is blurring between computers cell phones

447
00:34:36,141 --> 00:34:45,440
appliances and other electronics it's the continued shrinking of chip technology that's

448
00:34:45,441 --> 00:34:52,700
spurring the evolution what I'm holding up here is our manufacturing unit in silicon technology

449
00:34:52,701 --> 00:34:59,540
And this particular example is a wafer of Pentium III microprocessors.

450
00:34:59,800 --> 00:35:08,120
It's roughly 8 inches in diameter, and it holds over 200 Pentium III microprocessors.

451
00:35:12,640 --> 00:35:17,920
Each microprocessor can hold as many as 100 million tiny transistors.

452
00:35:17,921 --> 00:35:24,920
Chips are made by a process called photolithography.

453
00:35:24,921 --> 00:35:29,920
Chipmakers start by coating a silicon wafer with a photosensitive material.

454
00:35:29,921 --> 00:35:37,920
Scientists then make a stencil of the design they want by etching a thin metal film with an electron beam.

455
00:35:37,921 --> 00:35:45,920
Ultraviolet light shines through the stencil and onto the photosensitive chip,

456
00:35:45,921 --> 00:35:50,600
causing a chemical reaction and creating the circuitry for the layer.

457
00:35:52,720 --> 00:35:55,960
A chip is made by building layer upon layer.

458
00:35:58,640 --> 00:36:01,220
Those chips, which are basically mini computers,

459
00:36:01,740 --> 00:36:03,980
are finding their way into smart appliances.

460
00:36:05,440 --> 00:36:07,800
Discreet computers, hidden in everyday devices,

461
00:36:07,801 --> 00:36:12,300
are talking to each other via the web and by other wireless technologies.

462
00:36:12,301 --> 00:36:18,180
It turns out all of these devices, all of these things that are in the home, and literally

463
00:36:18,181 --> 00:36:22,360
you'll have a hundred different microprocessors in your home, are all now getting connected

464
00:36:22,361 --> 00:36:26,840
to the Internet, are all now getting connected to a home network, and are allowing people

465
00:36:26,841 --> 00:36:30,860
and things to access all of the resources that exist out on the Internet.

466
00:36:30,861 --> 00:36:35,620
But talking to your appliances via the telephone isn't a new idea.

467
00:36:35,621 --> 00:36:37,620
And this lady?

468
00:36:37,621 --> 00:36:39,620
She's calling her oven.

469
00:36:39,621 --> 00:36:40,620
Sound funny?

470
00:36:40,621 --> 00:36:43,620
Actually, it's something that could happen in the future,

471
00:36:43,621 --> 00:36:46,620
thanks to a Wesley House idea still in development

472
00:36:46,621 --> 00:36:50,620
